 of conscience you don't feel like  or to be pro-choice rather and you don't  feel like you can vote that we can you  tell us a little bit how you think  through that like what your role is as  an elected official on those issues of  faith well I pray a lot on it and I do  remember a situation it wasn't a  pro-life vote but it was a vote several  years ago that had a lot to do with an  issue that was considered anti Democrat  and as a result what happened was is  that I knew that if I voted this way it  would be very difficult for me and my  district would never understand this and  certainly the Democratic Party but it  was something that I felt very clear  about and was one of the and I was a  young believer in ice and I you know I I  really prayed about this and and said  God you you know what you're doing on  this because I think I'm leaning towards  voting this opposite way and this is  going to get me in a lot of trouble and  so it was one of the first times I  really trusted him and stepped out in it  well I voted against my party and it was  perceived as being against my party I  didn't I thought it was a very  principled vote and it had to do with  finances and financial matters and it  was a very very important bill back in  the late 80s anyway the next day the  newspaper said that Hall voted this way  and you know they editorial was the one  after me people went after me letters to  the editors and this guy's a bum bah bah  bah you know  and it was tough it was hard i went back  had a town hall meeting and i've never  seen this or ever heard this before but  I walked into the Town Hall me there's  250 people there soon as I walked in  they all stood up and booed me now I'm  thinking I'm in trouble my staff is  really nervous I'm a little bit nervous  this is the end of my career it's an  interesting thing though the next  campaign nobody ran against me I ran  unopposed so what's that tell you it  tells you that you know do the right  thing even though you