ng that's in front of us we can write  our congressmen and demand of our  congressmen not in a hateful way but in  a polite way and go in the same and said  see him or her and say look I care about  this issue I want you to care about this  issue and these are the bills I want you  to vote for and support American people  don't do that enough and let me tell you  as a elected official was 24 years in  the Congress when my constituents would  come to me and say this is important to  us we're watching your vote we need you  I always remembered that always remember  that  while you were remember the house two  times I think it was you made the  recommendation that the US should  apologize for slavery now that both the  House and the Senate have passed  resolutions apologizing for slavery was  not front-page news as you might recall  last year I wonder if you could just  talk a little bit about your perceptions  of how effective those resolutions were  and whether you think now with the  election of President Barack Obama we're  in a post-racial us well I don't know  about post-racial us but I do know that  you know when i introduced the apology  for slavery a few years ago I that was  one of the most difficult issues I ever  introduced because people were outraged  I mean I got more hate mail from around  the country and I mean it was some of  the most hideous things in the world  were said about me it was so I it opened  my eyes at at this problem that still is  brewing sometimes on the service but  well below the surface as well to how we  hate and the thinking of some of the  people in our country I was surprised i  also got some of the most beautiful  letters and some of the most warmest  thoughts and beautiful prayers that I  ever had we passed that I had I left  Congress and Congress eventually passed  that and it remains to be seen what is  going to happen with that I mean we did  apologize but nothing formally has  happened and I think the United States  is gra